{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789499852,"nonce":"iHS9llKAhhLMFL9S8TegqxrY4RU=","title":"A nested def that yields crashes, and takes the exception hooks with it","message":"```python\ndef over():\n    def g():\n        yield 1\n    for v in g():\n        print(v)\n    return 0\nprint(over())\n```\n\nDied with a signal. A nested `def` is lifted as a function value; one that yields was lifted with `is_fiber` false, so its yield ran outside any fiber, and calling it through the record called the body directly instead of starting a fiber. The failed lifting also left the module without the program's `zb_hook_raise` definition (the library's extern stayed an extern), so a raise-free program with a nested generator had no way to raise: an index error crashed, and the warm-up thread panicked compiling `zb_fatal` (\"can't resolve symbol zb_hook_raise\"). Why a lifted function that fails to lower drops the declarations after it, silently, is a lead of its own.\n\nNow a nested generator is a known closure like any nested def (its result is the generator, whatever its body returns); it is lifted as a fiber under the typed name with the environment prologue a module generator has, and a direct call starts the fiber with the record as the environment's head and the arguments after it (`start_generator_from`); the record names a starter that does the same for a call through a value. Conformance: generators/nested_def.py (arguments, captured cells, several starts of one def, next() on the generator).\n\nA generator as a dynamic value (stored in a list, passed as Any, iterated through `zb_any_iter`) is still not a thing: a Gen cast to Object is a bare fiber handle, not a box.","files":null}]}